; ds18b20.pe — a DS18B20 on 1-Wire, read by firmware.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY THIS ONE IS DIFFERENT FROM THE DHT11, AND WHY IT IS NEXT TO IT
;
; The DHT11 act samples BETWEEN two windows: a 0's release is over by 78 us and
; a 1's is not over until 120, so the host waits and looks. A DS18B20 is sampled
; INSIDE a slot, which inverts the whole problem:
;
;   WRITE (host -> sensor), one bit, LSB first:
;     a 1:  host low 1-15 us, then releases for the rest of the slot
;     a 0:  host low for the WHOLE slot (~60 us)
;   READ (sensor -> host), one bit, LSB first:
;     the sensor pulls low for 15-60 us (the slot's initiation), then releases.
;     A 0 leaves it high for the rest of the slot; a 1 pulls it low again for
;     1-15 us and then releases.
;
; So a read is not "wait N microseconds, read": the host must WAIT FOR THE
; SENSOR'S EDGES and then sample in a window that starts when the sensor's
; initiation pulse ends. And the bit order is LSB first, which is the opposite
; of the DHT11 — the same accumulator, used the other way round, and the mutation
; suite checks that getting it backwards is caught.
;
; It is also the only protocol here where the DEVICE initiates: after the host's
; reset pulse the sensor answers with a presence pulse, so the firmware is
; reading a line the sensor is driving.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE SEQUENCE, AND WHY EACH STEP IS A DIFFERENT KIND OF WAIT
;
;   1. RESET      host low >= 480 us.  A COUNTED DELAY: nothing on the wire
;                 says when 480 us is up, so this is the delay routine.
;   2. PRESENCE   the sensor pulls low 60-240 us, then releases.  A WAIT FOR AN
;                 EDGE: the host cannot know the sensor's timing, so it spins on
;                 the pin. The spin loop is why the pin matrix's read-back exists.
;   3. 0xCC       SKIP ROM — one addressed sensor, no 64-bit ROM code.  A WRITE:
;                 8 counted slots, each "low, then release" or "low, held".
;   4. 0xBE       READ SCRATCHPAD.  A write again.
;   5. 2 x 8      read slots.  A WAIT FOR AN EDGE then a counted delay then a
;                 sample — the hybrid that makes 1-Wire different from I2C.
;
; Steps 1, 3 and 4 are counted delays and step 5's inner delay is too; step 2 and
; the first half of step 5 are edge waits. That split is the lesson of the DHT11
; act, where the first design counted its way through the sensor's bit periods
; and drifted 40 us over twenty zero bits: where the wire announces an event, WAIT
; for the event; where it does not, COUNT.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; WHY TWO BYTES AND NOT NINE
;
; The scratchpad is nine bytes and the loop is the same for each; this reads the
; first two, which are the temperature, because the claim being made is about the
; SLOT TIMING and sixteen slots demonstrate that as well as seventy-two, at a
; third of the simulated time. The byte count is one number in dmem[3].
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE SLOTS, AND THE MARGIN IN THEM
;
;   write slot:  the datasheet's t0 is 1-15 us low + 60 us high (a 1) or 60 us
;                low (a 0). This program drives 5 us of low for a 1 and 65 us of
;                low for a 0, then a 15 us recovery.
;   read slot:   the sensor's initiation is 15-60 us low. The host spins for that
;                low, spins for the release, then waits 40 us and samples: at
;                40 us after the release a 0 is still high and a 1 has already
;                pulled the line low again for 1-15 us. So the 40 us sits inside
;                the window with 25 us of margin on the 0 side and 25 on the 1
;                side — the same balanced placement the DHT11 act arrived at, and
;                for the same reason.
;
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; THE PIN, THE OPEN-DRAIN QUESTION, AND WHY IT IS ANSWERED THE WAY IT IS
;
; 1-Wire is a single-wire bus where the host and the sensor both pull it low and
; a pull-up owns the high level. That is the OPEN-DRAIN case, and pe_pinmux has an
; OD bit precisely for it. This program does NOT use it, and the reason is worth
; stating because it looks like an omission:
;
;   the od bit makes a pin holding 1 RELEASE rather than drive high. During a READ
;   slot that is what we want (the line must float so the sensor can pull it),
;   and the firmware gets that by clearing PINOE instead — which is the same
;   electrical result, made explicit per bit. During a WRITE slot the host must
;   HOLD the line low for a 0, which od also allows. So od buys nothing here and
;   costs the one thing the pin read-back is for: with od set, a pin holding 1
;   reads back as RELEASED, so a write of 1 and a write of 0 look identical on the
;   pin. Keeping od clear means the host's own level is what the pad shows, and
;   the edge-wait loops see the sensor's pulses and nothing else. The i2c_pins.pe
;   argument — "write out=1 or out=0 and never touch oe or od again" — is the
;   right idiom for a bus where the level IS the data; 1-Wire is the opposite case,
;   where the data is a TIME and the level is just a carrier for it.
;
; bit 6 is the 1-Wire pin: the first unclaimed pad in the documented map.

        ; the pin and the slot constants are peasm CONSTS: OW_DATA, OW_RESET,
        ; OW_SLOT, OW_TSLOT. See tools/fw/peasm.py's timing block.

; ===========================================================================
; CLAIM THE PIN and take it off the pad: 1-Wire idles high.
; ===========================================================================
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; release every pin the reset had enabled
        LDI   A, OW_DATA
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; the line idles HIGH
        LDI   A, OW_DATA
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; claimed, and released (see the header: the
                                ; idle state of a 1-Wire bus is the pull-up)
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   12, A             ; the phase the delay returns to
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   14, A             ; the "done" flag, and the TB's observable

; ===========================================================================
; 1. THE RESET PULSE
; ===========================================================================
reset:  LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; the line goes LOW: the host's reset pulse
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 0
        LDI   A, OW_RST         ; 480 us of low. This is a COUNTED delay and it
        STM   9, A              ; has to be counted: nothing on the wire
        LDI   A, 4              ; announces that 480 us is up. The (4,40) pair
        STM   10, A             ; is the COARSE one (511 clocks = 8.5 us a step)
        LDI   A, 40
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 0: the reset is over; let the sensor answer ----
ph0:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; RELEASE: the sensor may now pull the line low
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 1 (the 15 us
        LDI   A, OW_T15         ; settle before the presence pulse is looked for)
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 2              ; the FINE pair from here on: 69 clocks
        STM   10, A             ; (1.22 us) a step, which is what a 5 us slot needs
        LDI   A, 13
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 1: wait for the sensor's PRESENCE pulse: low, then high ----
;
; The presence pulse is the sensor pulling low for 60-240 us and releasing. Two
; spin loops, and between them a counted 15 us is NOT used: the pulse's length
; is the sensor's to choose, so both edges are waited for. If the sensor is not
; there the first loop simply never exits, which is the correct behaviour for a
; bus with no timeout in this ISA — and the TB distinguishes "no sensor" from
; "sensor present" by whether the program got past this point.
ph1:    IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, OW_DATA
        JNZ   ph1               ; wait for LOW: the presence pulse's start
ph1b:   IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, OW_DATA
        JZ    ph1b              ; wait for HIGH: the pulse's end. Presence found.
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 2

; ===========================================================================
; WRITE A BYTE TO THE SENSOR (0xCC then 0xBE)
;
; dmem[13] holds the byte to send, dmem[2] the bit counter. The bits go LSB
; first, and the bit to send is decided by the value's own bit 0 — which is why
; this can be a loop with no shifting: read the byte, mask bit 0, send, then
; rotate the byte right so the next bit 0 is the next bit of the frame.
; ===========================================================================
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A             ; TWO command bytes to send: 0xCC then 0xBE
        LDI   A, 0xCC
        STM   6, A              ; SKIP ROM: one sensor on the bus
wbyte:  LDI   A, 8
        STM   2, A              ; eight bits
wbit:   LDM   A, 6             ; the byte to send lives in dmem[6],
        AND   A, 0x01           ; NOT 13: that is the delay's inner target
        JZ    wbit_zero

        ; ---- a 1: a short low, then release for the rest of the slot ----
        LDI   A, OW_DATA        ; 1-Wire writes DRIVE the line: release-then-
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; set-the-level does not pull it down, and the
        LDI   A, 0x00           ; pin is RELEASED here (ph0 released it), so
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; TXPIN=0 alone would leave the line HIGH and
                                ; the sensor would see a 1 for every bit. The
                                ; model caught this: it decoded 0 write bits
                                ; because the line never went low.
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 2
        LDI   A, OW_T5           ; 5 us of low: the datasheet's 1-15 us
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 13
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 2: the low pulse is over. For a 1 that means release; for a 0 the
;      program came here on a different path (wbit_zero below holds the line for
;      the whole slot), so the release has to be decided by the bit, not here. --
ph2:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; release: a 1 is a SHORT low, then the line
        LDI   A, 5              ; the delay returns to phase 5
        STM   12, A
        LDI   A, OW_T55         ; ...and the 1's high period is the rest of the
        STM   9, A              ; slot (~55 us). A previous version stored the
        LDI   A, 2              ; PHASE value 5 here as the counter, which made
        STM   10, A             ; the high period 4.6 us and ran the slots
        LDI   A, 13             ; together -- one of the three real bugs the
        STM   13, A             ; 1-Wire TB found
        JMP   dly1
ph5:    JMP   wbit_tail         ; the 55 us of high are over

wbit_zero:
        ; ---- a 0: hold the line low for the WHOLE slot ----
        LDI   A, OW_DATA        ; drive the line (see the 1-path note above)
        OUT   PINOE, A
        LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; and hold it low for the whole slot
        LDI   A, 3
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 3
        LDI   A, OW_T65         ; 65 us: the datasheet's ~60 us low for a 0
        STM   9, A
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 13
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 3: a 0's slot is over; release and move to the next bit ----
ph3:    LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; release: the next slot starts high

wbit_tail:
        ; ---- the inter-bit work: rotate the byte, count the bits ----
        LDM   A, 6             ; the byte being sent
        SHR   A                 ; the next bit of the frame is now bit 0
        STM   6, A
        LDM   A, 2              ; the bits left in this byte
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   2, A
        JNZ   wbit              ; more bits -> the next slot

        ; ---- the byte is sent. Which one is NEXT? ----
        ;
        ; dmem[10] counts the command bytes still to send: 2, then 1, then 0.
        ; After the decrement, 0 means BOTH 0xCC and 0xBE are out, so start the
        ; read. A previous version initialised this to 1 and decremented, so it
        ; went to the read after 0xCC and the sensor's second command was
        ; whatever the send register happened to hold. Both errors were found by
        ; the TB decoding the pads: "command 1: cc, command 2: 00".
        LDM   A, 10
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   10, A
        JZ    wr_done           ; both commands are out: start the read
        LDI   A, 0xBE           ; READ SCRATCHPAD, into the SEND register
        STM   6, A
        LDI   A, 8
        STM   2, A              ; eight more bits
        JMP   wbit
wr_done:
        LDI   A, 0
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; released: the sensor drives the read slots now

; ===========================================================================
; READ THE TEMPERATURE: two bytes, eight slots each.
;
; A read slot is: release, wait for the sensor's initiation low, wait for its
; release, wait 40 us, sample. The sample is 1 if the line has been pulled low
; again (the sensor's 1-15 us "1" pulse) and 0 if it is still high.
; ===========================================================================
        LDI   A, 8              ; EIGHT bits in this byte. This was 0 first,
        STM   3, A              ; so the first decrement underflowed to 255 and
        LDI   A, 2              ; the byte never banked: the 1-Wire TB saw 16
        STM   10, A             ; read slots and dmem[0..1] still unwritten.
                                ; The same bug as the WS2812 byte counter.

        LDI   A, 0
        STM   5, A              ; the byte index
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   4, A              ; the shift register
rbit:   LDI   A, OW_DATA        ; 1-Wire READS ARE HOST-INITIATED: the host
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; pulls the line low to ask for a bit, the
        LDI   A, 0x00           ; sensor answers with its own edge ~15 us
        OUT   TXPIN, A          ; later. A previous version only WAITED for
        LDI   A, 6              ; the sensor to start each slot, and a model
        STM   12, A             ; that drives slots on a timer never spoke to
        LDI   A, 6              ; it: 16 slots were sent and zero captured.
        STM   9, A              ; This is the datasheet's "initiation pulse".
                                ; Phase 6 (not 4) so it returns to ph4a -- the
                                ; release-and-wait -- and NOT to ph4, the
                                ; sample. The read loop and the write loop had
                                ; both claimed phases 4 and 5, so the read's
                                ; initiation delay jumped straight into the
                                ; sample, sampled 6 us in with the line still
                                ; low, and banked nothing.
        LDI   A, 2
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 13
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 4a: the initiation pulse is over; release and wait for the data ----
ph4a:   LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   PINOE, A          ; release: the sensor drives the data bit
ph4b:   IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, OW_DATA
        JNZ   ph4b              ; wait for the sensor's falling edge (its data)
                                ; This is the ONLY edge the read loop waits
                                ; for. A first version also waited for the
                                ; line to go HIGH again before sampling, and
                                ; that reads every bit as a 0: a 1's extra
                                ; low pulse is OVER by the time the release
                                ; arrives, so "wait for high, then sample"
                                ; can only ever see the 0 case. The 1 and
                                ; the 0 differ INSIDE the slot -- a 1 HOLDS
                                ; the line low where a 0 releases -- so the
                                ; sample has to land in that window, which
                                ; means waiting a fixed time after the
                                ; initiation edge, not waiting for a release
                                ; that means opposite things per bit.
        LDI   A, 4
        STM   12, A             ; the delay returns to phase 4
        LDI   A, OW_T40         ; sample this far after the sensor's edge:
        STM   9, A              ; a 0 has released (high), a 1 still holds
        LDI   A, 2              ; the line low
        STM   10, A
        LDI   A, 13
        STM   13, A
        JMP   dly1

; ---- phase 4: SAMPLE ONCE, and shift it in LSB first ----
;
; The accumulator shifts LEFT with the new bit at bit 0, so the FIRST bit read
; ends up as the LEAST significant — which is 1-Wire's order and the opposite of
; the DHT11's. The same two instructions, the opposite meaning: this is the one
; place in the project where a shared accumulator is used backwards on purpose,
; and the mutation suite checks that a firmware which shifts the other way (and
; so decodes a byte-reversed frame) is caught rather than accepted.
ph4:    IN    A, PIN
        AND   A, OW_DATA
        JZ    rb_was_zero
        LDM   A, 4
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X              ; a = 2*acc: the accumulator shifts LEFT
        OR    A, 0x01           ; ...and a 1 goes in as the new bit 0
        STM   4, A
        JMP   rb_taken
rb_was_zero:
        LDM   A, 4
        MOV   X, A
        ADD   A, X              ; a 0: the same shift, bit 0 left clear
        STM   4, A
rb_taken:
        LDM   A, 3
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   3, A
        JNZ   rbit_done         ; more bits in this byte
        ; ---- the byte is complete: bank it, next byte or done ----
        LDM   A, 5
        MOV   X, A
        LDM   A, 4
        STS   [X], A            ; dmem[0..1]: the temperature, LSB byte first
        LDI   A, 0
        STM   4, A
        LDM   A, 5
        ADD   A, 1
        STM   5, A
        LDM   A, 10
        SUB   A, 1
        STM   10, A
        JZ    all_done
        LDI   A, 8
        STM   3, A
rbit_done:
        JMP   rbit              ; straight back into the edge waits

all_done:
        LDI   A, 1
        STM   14, A             ; both bytes are in -- the TB's observable
park:   LDI   A, 0x00
        OUT   TXPIN, A
halt:   JMP   halt              ; the ISA has no HALT

; ===========================================================================
; THE DELAY -- the same three nested counters as servo_sweep.pe and
; dht11_read.pe, with the same reload trap in it.
;
;     total(n1,n2,n3) = (n1-1) * (10 + (n2-1) * (4*n3 + 7)) + 4   clocks
;
; (n2,n3) = (4,40) throughout: one outer step is 10 + 3*167 = 511 clocks =
; 8.5 us, which is the right resolution for slots of 5, 15, 40 and 65 us and
; for the 480 us reset.
; ===========================================================================
dly1:   LDM   A, 9              ;  1  the outer counter
        SUB   A, 1              ;  2
        STM   9, A              ;  3
        JZ    dly_done          ;  4
        LDM   A, 10             ;  5  the middle counter's target
        STM   11, A             ;  6  into the loop's working copy
dly2:   LDM   A, 11             ;  7  the middle counter
        SUB   A, 1              ;  8
        STM   11, A             ;  9
        JZ    dly1              ; 10
        LDM   A, 13             ; 11  the inner counter's target
        STM   15, A             ; 12  into the loop's working copy
dly3:   LDM   A, 15             ; 13  the inner counter
        SUB   A, 1              ; 14
        STM   15, A             ; 15
        JNZ   dly3              ; 16
        JMP   dly2              ; 17
dly_done:
        ; The ISA cannot return to seven addresses, so the phase in dmem[12]
        ; chooses where. Two bugs lived here, both from patching one dispatch
        ; for two different loops: it first handled 0-3 and sent everything >= 4
        ; to ph4, which dropped a write-1's high period into the read path; and
        ; when the read loop was given phases 4 and 5 it collided with the write
        ; loop's 4 and 5. A dispatch is a FUNCTION over every phase a program
        ; uses, so it has to be written against the full phase set at once --
        ; adding a phase means extending the chain, not reusing a number.
        LDM   A, 12
        JZ    ph0               ; 0: the reset is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph1               ; 1: the presence wait
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph2               ; 2: a write-1's low pulse is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph3               ; 3: a write-0's low slot is over
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph4               ; 4: a read slot's sample instant
        SUB   A, 1
        JZ    ph5               ; 5: a write-1's high period is over
        JMP   ph4a              ; 6: a read slot's initiation pulse is over
